Two evaluation updates: Langfuse links batch results to the run that produced them, while Phoenix fixes how prompt formats and client settings are applied. All three discovery channels were checked; this edition retains two GitHub releases.

## Langfuse groups batch-evaluation traces and scores by run

Source: Langfuse — https://github.com/langfuse/langfuse/releases/tag/v4.70.0
Published: 2026-10-24
Channel: github

Langfuse records AI application activity and scores it with evaluators, which are saved checks of output quality or behavior. When engineers run those checks over many existing records, they need to distinguish that batch’s results from other evaluations. Version 4.70.0 adds batch_action_id metadata to each execution trace and score produced by a batch run. A trace records the work performed during an execution; the shared identifier lets operators find one batch’s outputs together.

For illustration, a team could rerun a revised answer-quality check over yesterday’s assistant responses. Grouping the resulting scores and evaluation traces by batch would help reviewers inspect that particular rerun. The identifier establishes which run produced a record; it does not establish that the evaluator’s judgement is correct.

The release also adds runBatchEvaluation through Model Context Protocol, an interface that lets agents call tools. The linked implementation limits tool availability to deployments with the preview opt-in enabled, requires evaluation permissions, and applies the plan’s data-access window. Langfuse’s in-app agent asks for approval before this tool runs. These are implementation conditions, rather than a claim that every deployment exposes the feature.

Why it matters: For Langfuse evaluation owners: verify a small batch’s traces and scores share its identifier, and check preview availability, permissions and the accessible date range before automating reruns.

